        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">Tenant calls vs. leasing calls, and why they need different handling</h2>
        <p>
          A property phone carries two businesses. One is operations: the person who already
          lives there and has a problem. The other is leasing: the person who might sign.
          If both calls land in the same script, you either wake a plumber for a tour request
          or lose a prospect while you troubleshoot a toilet.
        </p>
        <p>You write two paths. The setup follows the one the caller is actually on.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Tenant call.</strong> Property, unit, name, and the problem. Then your emergency tree. A maintenance issue that can wait gets a window and a text confirmation. An emergency goes to the vendor and the on-call manager you named for that property.</li>
          <li><strong>Leasing call.</strong> Move-in timing, bedrooms, pets, and whatever else you require before a showing. A qualified prospect gets a real slot. A caller who is outside your criteria gets a clear no, not a tour you will cancel later.</li>
          <li><strong>Neither.</strong> Owners, vendors, and existing applicants with a status question do not get the tenant script or the leasing script. You name who those calls reach, and the setup routes them there or takes a detailed message and texts it.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Northwest Arkansas tenants call in English and in Spanish. A setup that only handles
          one of those is not answering for the portfolio you actually manage. What our build
          can and cannot do in Spanish, today, is something we will demonstrate on the
          assessment call rather than promise in print.
        </p>
        <p>
          The machinery is the same
          <a class="text-link" href="/ai-receptionist/">AI receptionist</a>
          we build for other Arkansas businesses. The rules are property rules: unit, vendor
          list, and which calendar a showing lands on. It is not a trade script with the
          word "tenant" swapped in.
        </p>

        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">Emergency triage for NWA properties: burst pipe vs. a running toilet</h2>
        <p>
          Both calls involve water. They are not the same job. You write the line before
          the first after-hours call, and the setup does not move a call across that line
          on its own.
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Active water damage.</strong> A burst pipe, a supply-line break, or any fixture that is wetting floors, cabinets, or the unit below. Emergency. The setup reaches the plumber on your list for that property and texts your on-call manager the unit, the caller, and what is flooding. Same path at 2 p.m. and at 2 a.m.</li>
          <li><strong>A fixture that can wait.</strong> A running toilet that is not overflowing, a dripping faucet, a slow drain. Maintenance. The setup logs the unit and the symptom, tells the tenant the window you defined, and puts the work on the vendor's next slot. It does not wake a plumber for a flapper.</li>
          <li><strong>The facts changed.</strong> A running toilet that starts overflowing moves to the emergency path. A burst pipe the tenant has already shut off, with no active flooding, stays on the maintenance path if that is the rule you wrote. The caller is asked those facts. The setup does not guess.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          HVAC and electrical use the same split, with thresholds you set. No heat, a burning
          smell, or a whole-building outage dispatches now. A thermostat question, or a single
          dead outlet that is not a safety issue, waits for the next window. You decide where
          a no-cool call in July sits. Benton County and Washington County both produce these
          calls. The tree is what keeps a maintenance ticket from burning an emergency
          dispatch, and an emergency from sitting in a morning inbox.
        </p>
        <p>
          Every emergency call is recorded and transcribed, the same as every other call.
          If you do not want a gate code or a lockbox combination spoken on a recording,
          do not put it in the script. Pass access the way you already trust.
        </p>

        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">After-hours maintenance dispatch to your vendor list</h2>
        <p>
          We do not send our own plumbers, HVAC techs, or electricians. Dispatch uses the
          list you already run across Benton County and Washington County: who is first
          call for a given property, who is backup, and which number is the after-hours
          number rather than the shop line that rolls to voicemail.
        </p>
        <p>On an emergency, the handoff carries four things:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Property and unit, plus the caller's name and callback number</li>
          <li>What is failing, in the words the triage questions captured</li>
          <li>Whether the tenant is still in the unit, and any access note you approved for that property</li>
          <li>Which vendor was reached, and whether they accepted, so your on-call manager is not guessing in the morning</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Bentonville and Rogers have been adding multifamily along the Pinnacle Hills
          corridor. More doors on that side of the metro means more after-hours maintenance
          calls that are not in Fayetteville. The vendor list has to know the difference.
          A plumber who covers west Fayetteville is not automatically the plumber for a
          stack in Rogers, and the setup will not assume they are.
        </p>
        <p>
          Routine work does not use the emergency numbers. It goes on the next maintenance
          window you defined, and the tenant gets a text with that window. Your coordinator
          still owns vendor relationships, owner approvals, and the exceptions the script
          was not written for. The phone layer covers the ring they cannot take.
        </p>

        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">Showing scheduling and prospect qualification</h2>
        <p>
          Leasing calls fail in a specific way in this metro. The University of Arkansas
          anchors Fayetteville's rental market, the region has about 37,000 college students,
          and Northwest Arkansas Community College adds its own rental cycle in Fayetteville
          and Springdale. Showing requests and lease questions bunch up before the fall
          semester. Move-out and turnover calls bunch up at the end of spring. Those weeks
          do not match office hours, and they land on the same phone as a burst pipe.
        </p>
        <p>
          Qualification is the list you already use, asked on the call: desired move-in,
          bedrooms, pets, occupant count, and whether the caller is inside the criteria
          for that property. A prospect who clears the list gets a showing on the calendar
          you already use. A prospect who does not gets a direct answer, not a hold message.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you would rather have a qualified prospect pick the slot than wait on a
          callback, that is what
          <a class="text-link" href="/booking-software/">our booking software</a>
          is for: a real open window, on your calendar, without a second inbox. It does
          not replace the guest card in your property software. It stops the tour from
          depending on someone being free to call back.
        </p>
        <p>
          Fayetteville is the center of that student cycle, and it is also where a lot of
          non-property businesses start with us. The city answering page, which is not
          this property-management setup, is
          <a class="text-link" href="/answering-service-fayetteville-ar/">answering service for Fayetteville, AR</a>.
        </p>

        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">Works with AppFolio, Buildium, Yardi, and Propertyware workflows</h2>
        <p>
          AppFolio, Buildium, Yardi, and Propertyware already split the work this phone
          has to split: tenant maintenance versus leasing, emergency versus routine,
          which vendor, which showing calendar. The answering setup follows that split.
          It does not replace the system you pay for, and we do not claim a certified
          or native sync into any of them.
        </p>
        <p>What "works with" means, in practice:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>The greeting, the questions, and the emergency tree match the categories your coordinators already use in that software.</li>
          <li>A maintenance call ends as a transcript and a text your office can turn into the work order. A leasing call ends as a showing on your calendar, or a message with the answers your guest-card process needs.</li>
          <li>You decide what is allowed to be said on a recorded line. Owner ledgers, full application packages, and account numbers stay in the property system.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          If a caller needs something the script cannot see, the balance on a ledger,
          an application sitting in review, an owner approval that has not come back,
          the call goes to the person you named. The setup does not invent an answer
          from a system it is not logged into.
        </p>

